---
scope: src/app/learn/spec-kit-alternatives
alignment: unreviewed
description: The /learn page answering spec-kit alternatives and spec-kit vs OpenSpec, with an honest feature table of the spec-driven tools and intent-driven AIDE, and who each one fits.
intent: >
  This page answers people searching 'spec-kit alternatives' and 'spec-kit vs
  openspec'. It compares spec-kit, OpenSpec, BMAD, superpowers, Kiro, Tessl,
  Agent OS, Task Master and intent-driven AIDE in a table on concrete
  dimensions, then says briefly who each tool fits. Every competitor is
  described fairly and accurately, every fact traces to the research, and
  GitHub star counts carry a visible 'as of' date. AIDE is one option among
  them, not the only right answer. Readers leave able to choose, with links on
  to the spec vs intent comparison and the definition page.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The H1 and an H2 carry the searched phrases 'spec-kit alternatives' and 'spec-kit vs OpenSpec', and the page opens with a direct answer before any table or section.
    - A feature table compares all nine tools on concrete dimensions, with any fact the research does not establish marked unknown in its cell.
    - Every GitHub star count shown carries a visible 'as of' date and matches the research figure for that date; a tool with no figure in the research shows unknown.
    - A short take on each tool says who it fits, in terms that tool's own users would recognise, with AIDE presented as one option among them.
    - The page links on to the spec-driven vs intent-driven comparison page and the intent-driven engineering definition page.
  undesired:
    - A competitor misdescribed, given a feature it lacks, denied one it has, or written off as abandoned on evidence the research marks unverified.
    - A guessed cell, star count or date filled in where the research has no figure, instead of a cell marked unknown.
    - A star count with no 'as of' date, or one that is refreshed automatically rather than entered by hand from a dated source.
    - A page framed as AIDE versus everyone, where every row favours AIDE or AIDE is presented as the only right choice.
    - Headings in house wording rather than the searched phrases, or an answer buried under preamble.
    - A tool from the nine left out of the table or the take on who each fits.
---
